    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"Selected elements from the Hydrogeochemical Stream Sediment\r\nReconnaissance database of the National Uranium Resource Evaluation program are\r\ndiagnostic and may predict the presence of commercially important heavy minerals.\r\nTitanium, hafnium, zirconium, rare-earth elements (REE: Ce, Dy, Eu, La, Lu, Sm,\r\nYb, Y), thorium, and uranium are among the elements analyzed and reported in the\r\nNURE database. Examples from the southeastern and northwestern states show\r\nthat concentrations of these elements in samples can predict the presence of\r\ncommercially important heavy minerals such as ilmenite, rutile, zircon, monazite,\r\nand xenotime. Ilmenite and rutile are the most commonly occurring Ti-bearing\r\nminerals in sediments. Zirconium and hafnium possess extraordinary geochemical\r\naffinity; thus, the presence of Hf (or of Zr) usually indicates the presence of zircon.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
